{"content_hash": "892a68ae019baa83", "version_id": 132, "article_id": 14, "level_code": "en_a2", "lang": "en", "level": 2, "level_label": "A2 初级", "title": "Queen Camilla says goodbye to writer Jeffrey Archer", "title_zh": "卡米拉王后告别作家杰弗里·阿彻", "topic": "culture", "source": "bbc_culture", "source_url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cm3wvq4jj64yo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/standard/240/cpsprodpb/3d1d/live/ac47b690-c188-11f1-83c7-97e75190a976.jpg", "credit": ""}, "published_date": "2026-10-06", "word_count": 175, "reading_minutes": 0.9, "lead": "Jeffrey Archer was a famous British writer and politician. He died at 86. Queen Camilla and many other people said nice things about him.", "lead_zh": "杰弗里·阿彻是英国著名的作家和政治人物。他去世了，享年86岁。卡米拉王后和其他许多人都说了他的好话。", "preview": "Jeffrey Archer died on Monday at his home. He was 86 years old. He was a writer and a politician. He sold more than 300 million books. His l", "preview_zh": "杰弗里·阿彻周一在家中去世，享年86岁。他既是作家，也是政治家。他的书卖出了超过3亿本。他最后一本书《亚当与夏娃》在周二出版。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 122, "url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@245c36d/content/audio/14/en_a2.mp3", "duration": 73.138, "size_bytes": 442656, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural", "word_count": 202, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@245c36d/content/data/versions/132.json", "paragraphs": ["Jeffrey Archer died on Monday at his home. He was 86 years old. He was a writer and a politician. He sold more than 300 million books. His last book, Adam and Eve, came out on Tuesday.", "Queen Camilla said she was sad to hear the news. She said Archer was full of life. She said he was not perfect, but he was a special person. She sent her love to his wife Mary and their family.", "Archer was born in London in 1940. He became a Conservative MP in 1969. Later, he went to the House of Lords. But in 2001, he went to prison for four years. He lied in a court case. He left prison in 2003 and returned to the Lords. He retired in 2024.", "Many people remembered Archer. Kemi Badenoch said his death was very sad. John Major said he lived a remarkable life. Gyles Brandreth said Archer was a nicer person after prison. Archer and his wife were married for 60 years. They have two sons and five grandchildren."], "paragraphs_zh": ["杰弗里·阿彻周一在家中去世，享年86岁。他既是作家，也是政治家。他的书卖出了超过3亿本。他最后一本书《亚当与夏娃》在周二出版。", "卡米拉王后说，听到这个消息她很难过。她说阿彻充满活力。她说他并不完美，但他是个特别的人。她向他的妻子玛丽和家人表达了慰问。", "阿彻1940年出生于伦敦。1969年，他成为保守党议员。后来，他进入了上议院。但在2001年，他入狱四年，因为他在一场官司中撒了谎。2003年他出狱，回到上议院。2024年他退休。", "很多人都怀念阿彻。凯米·巴德诺赫说他的去世令人非常难过。约翰·梅杰说他的一生非凡。贾尔斯·布兰德雷斯说，阿彻出狱后变成了更好的人。阿彻和妻子结婚60年，有两个儿子和五个孙子孙女。"], "body": "Jeffrey Archer died on Monday at his home. He was 86 years old. He was a writer and a politician. He sold more than 300 million books. His last book, Adam and Eve, came out on Tuesday.\n\nQueen Camilla said she was sad to hear the news. She said Archer was full of life. She said he was not perfect, but he was a special person. She sent her love to his wife Mary and their family.\n\nArcher was born in London in 1940. He became a Conservative MP in 1969. Later, he went to the House of Lords. But in 2001, he went to prison for four years. He lied in a court case. He left prison in 2003 and returned to the Lords. He retired in 2024.\n\nMany people remembered Archer. Kemi Badenoch said his death was very sad. John Major said he lived a remarkable life. Gyles Brandreth said Archer was a nicer person after prison. Archer and his wife were married for 60 years. They have two sons and five grandchildren.", "body_zh": "杰弗里·阿彻周一在家中去世，享年86岁。他既是作家，也是政治家。他的书卖出了超过3亿本。他最后一本书《亚当与夏娃》在周二出版。\n\n卡米拉王后说，听到这个消息她很难过。她说阿彻充满活力。她说他并不完美，但他是个特别的人。她向他的妻子玛丽和家人表达了慰问。\n\n阿彻1940年出生于伦敦。1969年，他成为保守党议员。后来，他进入了上议院。但在2001年，他入狱四年，因为他在一场官司中撒了谎。2003年他出狱，回到上议院。2024年他退休。\n\n很多人都怀念阿彻。凯米·巴德诺赫说他的去世令人非常难过。约翰·梅杰说他的一生非凡。贾尔斯·布兰德雷斯说，阿彻出狱后变成了更好的人。阿彻和妻子结婚60年，有两个儿子和五个孙子孙女。", "text": "Jeffrey Archer died on Monday at his home. He was 86 years old. He was a writer and a politician. He sold more than 300 million books. His last book, Adam and Eve, came out on Tuesday. Queen Camilla said she was sad to hear the news. She said Archer was full of life. She said he was not perfect, but he was a special person. She sent her love to his wife Mary and their family. Archer was born in London in 1940. He became a Conservative MP in 1969. Later, he went to the House of Lords. But in 2001, he went to prison for four years. He lied in a court case. He left prison in 2003 and returned to the Lords. He retired in 2024. Many people remembered Archer. Kemi Badenoch said his death was very sad. John Major said he lived a remarkable life. Gyles Brandreth said Archer was a nicer person after prison. Archer and his wife were married for 60 years. They have two sons and five grandchildren.", "vocab": [{"word": "writer", "pos": "noun", "zh": "作家", "note": ""}, {"word": "politician", "pos": "noun", "zh": "政治家", "note": ""}, {"word": "die", "pos": "verb", "zh": "去世", "note": "过去式是 died"}, {"word": "prison", "pos": "noun", "zh": "监狱", "note": ""}, {"word": "lie", "pos": "verb", "zh": "撒谎", "note": "过去式是 lied"}, {"word": "tribute", "pos": "noun", "zh": "致敬；悼念", "note": "pay tribute to 表示致敬"}, {"word": "remarkable", "pos": "adj", "zh": "非凡的", "note": ""}, {"word": "retire", "pos": "verb", "zh": "退休", "note": ""}], "title_original": "Queen pays tribute to 'irrepressible' Jeffrey Archer", "summary_original": "The bestselling author and former politician died on Monday at the age of 86.", "body_original": "Queen pays tribute to 'irrepressible' Jeffrey Archer\nBy\nEmma Saunders\n,\nCulture reporter\nand\nHenry Moore\nPublished\n6 October 2026\nQueen Camilla has paid tribute to the bestselling author and former politician Jeffrey Archer, who has died at the age of 86.\n\"Seldom does an author get to live a life as rich, varied and colourful as any of the characters in their books, or with as many surprising plot twists,\" the Queen said in a statement.\nShe added that Archer was \"not without flaw\", but that \"friends will recall him as an irrepressible life force through all the many differing chapters of his life\".\nThe former Conservative MP was jailed for perjury in 2001 after being found guilty of lying and cheating in his 1987 libel case against the Daily Star.\nThe Queen said he was \"an exception to the end\" and that it was \"with sadness that I learned of his death yesterday, on the eve of publication of his final work and so soon after appearing at my Queen's Reading Room Festival\".\nArcher was a supporter of the Queen's Reading Room charity.\n\"My sympathies go to Mary and the family at this time of sorrow. I hope it may be some consolation for them to know what pleasure Jeffrey brought to me and to so many millions of readers through the pages of his works.\n\"Wherever the next page turns to, I trust there will be champagne and Shepherd's Pie in abundance. Camilla R.\"\nArcher's parties were known for serving champagne and shepherd's pie to guests.\nFalse alibi\nArcher sold more than 300 million books in his five-decade writing career, including the Clifton Chronicles and Kane and Abel, and often set the novels in his home town of Wellington, Somerset.\nIn a statement, Archer's publisher HarperCollins said the author died \"suddenly and peacefully at home\" on Monday between writing sessions.\nArcher was born in north London in 1940 but soon moved to Weston-super-Mare in Somerset and in 1969, Archer was elected MP for the Lincolnshire constituency of Louth.\nHe served as an MP for five years before becoming Conservative Party deputy chairman.\nHe moved to the House of Lords in 1992 but was jailed for four years for perjury and perverting the course of justice in 2001.\nHe was found to have lied in libel action he had brought against the Daily Star newspaper, in which he was awarded £500,000 in damages.\nThe Star had alleged Archer had slept with a sex worker.\nThe peer was found by the court to have perverted the course of justice by asking a former friend to give him a false alibi and producing fake diary entries to back up his story.\nHe was also found to have perjured himself on oath during the libel trial.\nHe was released from jail in 2003 and returned to the House of Lords until he retired in 2024.\nConservative Party leader Kemi Badenoch described Archer's death as \"devastating news\".\n\"We will miss him very much,\" she told BBC Breakfast on Tuesday, adding that he had texted her earlier on Monday.\nBadenoch said he was \"very much loved by the Conservative family\", adding: \"I'm glad that I saw him recently.\"\nSpeaking about what she described as \"other parts of his life\", she said: \"He did serve his time, he made amends, and he wanted to do a lot of good in the world.\"\nFormer Conservative prime minister Sir John Major said \"we have lost a man who lived one of the most remarkable lives of our age\", and that he will be \"much missed – and long remembered\".\nArcher's last novel, Adam and Eve, was released on Tuesday.\nBroadcaster Gyles Brandreth told BBC Breakfast that he was with Archer on Sunday. \"I mean, literally 72 hours ago, doing a show in the West End of London celebrating this book, the publication, and he was in great form.\n\"He didn't really acknowledge that he'd changed much since being in prison, but I think he became a nicer person in some ways, a gentler, a milder person after the prison experience.\"\nFormer Conservative Party leader Iain Duncan Smith described Archer as \"a remarkable man who did everything at full bore\", adding: \"There were simply no half measures with him.\"\nOther former ministers also paid tribute. Sajid Javid said Archer had been a \"dear friend\", while Sir Jeremy Hunt said Archer had \"showed enormous kindness with his advice about how to become a writer\".\nJames Cleverly said the author and politician had been \"a huge personality and fantastic storyteller\", and that he had been very supportive when his wife Suzie was recovering from cancer.\nReform UK member and former Conservative minister Nadim Zahawi, wrote: \"Jeffrey Archer was a dear friend and an extraordinary human being. His gift for storytelling was matched by his wit, warmth and boundless energy. Life was never dull in Jeffrey's company.\"\nAuthor and former politician Jeffrey Archer dies aged 86\nPublished\n15 hours ago\nJeffrey Archer: bestselling novelist whose political career ended in scandal\nPublished\n1 day ago\nArcher 'nostalgic' about Somerset before death\nPublished\n14 hours ago\nLast week, Archer returned to his former school to promote his last book.\n\"It's wonderful to come back. What I got a real thrill about when I was walking towards the library is seeing the young boys and girls and thinking, 'that's the next generation',\" Archer said at Wellington School.\nArcher had been due to attend the Cheltenham Literature Festival on Saturday to discuss his final novel.\nHe and his wife Mary had celebrated their 60th wedding anniversary this year, HarperCollins said.\nThe couple have two sons and five grandchildren.\nRelated topics\nQueen Camilla\nUK Royal Family\nLiterature\nBooks", "fetched_at": "2026-10-07T00:55:38.692252", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "Jeffrey", "s": 0.1, "e": 0.575, "sm": 100, "em": 575, "cs": 0, "ce": 7, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "Archer", "s": 0.588, "e": 0.988, "sm": 587, "em": 987, "cs": 8, "ce": 14, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "died", "s": 1.0, "e": 1.288, "sm": 1000, "em": 1287, "cs": 15, "ce": 19, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": "on", "s": 1.3, "e": 1.413, "sm": 1300, "em": 1412, "cs": 20, "ce": 22, "si": 0}, {"i": 4, "w": "Monday", "s": 1.425, "e": 1.85, "sm": 1425, "em": 1850, "cs": 23, "ce": 29, "si": 0}, {"i": 5, "w": "at", "s": 1.863, "e": 1.963, "sm": 1862, "em": 1962, "cs": 30, "ce": 32, "si": 0}, {"i": 6, "w": "his", "s": 1.975, "e": 2.113, "sm": 1975, "em": 2112, "cs": 33, "ce": 36, "si": 0}, {"i": 7, "w": "home", "s"